  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard or waited for the rule to run, so neither has been checked beyond the tools accepting them.\n\n**Fields:** The data view `kibana_sample_data_logs` uses `timestamp` as its time field. The fields I used are `response` (a string, so I used `response.keyword`), `extension.keyword` and `bytes`. The rest describe the client and geography: `clientip`, `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `machine.os`, `agent`, `url`, `request`, `referer`, `host`, `tags`.\n\n**Dashboard:** `EFF-5f350ac8 Web logs overview` has four panels and a default range of the last 7 days. It's at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-5f350ac8-web-logs-overview-822520934bd5. The panels are:\n- Total requests (single number)\n- Requests over time (line)\n- Requests by response code (pie)\n- Average bytes by extension (bar)\n\n**Alert rule:** `EFF-5f350ac8 5xx spike` (id `20d8891c-003d-41c6-aab8-d22bcc327525`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It checks every minute. It fires when more than 10 documents in the last 5 minutes have a `response.keyword` matching `5xx`. It has no action attached, so it will show up in Kibana's alert list but won't notify anyone.\n\nThe rule was created disabled by default. I enabled it because you asked for it to fire on a spike. The sample logs are historical, so it will only trigger if recent 5xx documents are indexed.",
